  "72": {
   "text": "¹ The Russians call by this name the village and the river Iˊlpi, and also the whole country occupied",
   "edited": false
  },
  "73": {
   "text": "by that division of the Kerek. The proper river Хатырка, however, lies farther to the east. Its Kerek name is",
   "edited": false
  },
  "74": {
   "text": "Hatɪˊykan or Hatɪˊrkan. On the map the Chukchee variety of the name (Vatɪˊrkan) is given.",
   "edited": false
  },
  "75": {
   "text": "² On Russian and English maps, Cape Anannon, through a general misunderstanding, has been called",
   "edited": false
  },
  "76": {
   "text": "Cape Alutorsky; and the real Cape Alutorsky, Cape Govensky.",
   "edited": false
  },
  "77": {
   "text": "³ Chukchee. The meaning is the same as in Russian.",
   "edited": false
  },
  "78": {
   "text": "⁴ This name, although belonging to a Yukaghir village, is Lamut. It comes from a mountain near by,",
   "edited": false
  },
  "79": {
   "text": "which has the shape of a boat.",
   "edited": false
  },
  "80": {
   "text": "⁵ Yukaghir, meaning “stony.” The first Russian name is probably a corruption of it. It is, however,",
   "edited": false
  },
  "81": {
   "text": "unknown even to the Russian inhabitants of the country, though it appears on most of the maps. The second",
   "edited": false
  },
  "82": {
   "text": "Russian name means “dry river.” The Lamut name of the river is Buks˙unda, which means “icy river.” Of",
   "edited": true,
   "note": "that's a raised dot.\n\nLamut (Even) should be classed as a tier-1 language"
  },
  "83": {
   "text": "this last some travellers have made, through mispronunciation, Buyunda, though the real river of this name lies",
   "edited": false
  },
  "84": {
   "text": "much farther to the south.",
   "edited": false
  }
 }
